bashir rahama is still searching for  what he can salvage from his home it  collapsed on tuesday after flash floods  in mikaela in northern sudan's river now  state it's the result of days of heavy  rains in the region and rain water from  the east overflowing streams in the town  the rain started in the morning it  stopped raining for hours and then while  we were opening the streams for water to  pass the floods hit us it didn't stop  till the next morning the rain waters  usually just flow through the stream but  this time we went over it and no barrier  or stream was able to stop it  bashir's home was one of more than  seventeen hundred homes destroyed by the  floods in mikhail uptown alone more than  three thousand homes were damaged in  berber county and what many here say has  been the worst destruction they've seen  mahmoud abdullah shows us what remains  of his once large family home where  rooms once stood now only rubble remains  there were two bedrooms there another  two on the other side and a kitchen  there  the water came and in less than an hour  the houses collapsed we didn't even have  a chance to take out anything because we  were busy making sure that people got  out alive  some have left their homes to seek  shelter after losing nearly everything  but many others have stayed behind  the destruction has left families out in  the open many say they'll wait here  until the end of the rainy season to  rebuild their homes but that may take  some time because more rain is forecast  in the coming days and there are  concerns about waterborne diseases  the rain and flood waters have damaged  sewage systems and it's hard to find  drinking water in some parts of the town  my life  there is no access to clean drinking  water or to a health clinic my daughter  is now sick in a tent nearby while we'll  try to salvage what we can but there is  no medical treatment available  at least 15 people have been killed as a  result of heavy rains and flash floods  around sudan those who survived say  they're not sure how they'll be able to  rebuild their lives after losing so much  well that was hibern reporting from  berber and just a short while ago she  sent us this update  the start of the rainy season in some  parts of the country it's already made  rainy season in other parts of the  country but the devastation is now  growing wider and wider to include more  counties around the country and here in  berber  where people have lost most of their  positions they're still waiting for  assistance people have been living out  in the open they're trying to cope with  what has happened  on tuesday some of them saying that  they're still trying to take out the  positions that they can  salvage from the ruins trying to find  higher places away from the flood waters  because they're expecting more rains in  the coming days now they say that they  have not seen this kind of destruction  before therefore  they're not sure how to cope with the  situation they've lost  the local market where they used to buy  uh their vegetables and their food they  still don't have access to clean water  and they're still lacking access to  health care and medication so they're  saying that it's very hard to deal with  the current situation in light of the  fact that this is the first time it's  happening for to them well the  government government has created an  emergency room for uh the state as well  as for other states around the country  to deal with the issues of flooding  flash flooding and the displacement of  people and losing their properties but  the issue is especially here in mikaela  in berber county people say they've been  waiting for three days for tents because  they're now out in the open and that has  not been distributed out to them they  say that they're expecting more rains in  the coming days so they don't know how  they'll be able to deal with that  situation many of them still out in the  open many of them still waiting to be 